  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 314 AM EDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nRather unsurprising, the upper level pattern going into the long \nterm outlook (beginning Sat) is dependent on the speed at which the \neastern CONUS trough exits the area. Ensemble analysis shows the \nmajority (68%) of members from GEFS and ENS move the trough out on \nSaturday, with only 6-12% holding the trough into Late \nSaturday/early Sunday. What this means is one last enhanced day for \nupper level enhancement of precip, before the ridge moves overhead. \nGiven PWATs over 2\" and slow moving/greater coverage of storms, \nexcessive rainfall is possible into Saturday. This will be \nespecially true for areas which see heavy rain in days prior.\n\nAs the ridge moves overhear, conditions will transition more to a \ntypical summertime pattern with highs in the 90s and heat indices \nback in the triple digits. While afternoon thunderstorms coverage \nwon't be nearly as high, we will likely see disorganized diurnal \nconvection each afternoon through next week. As is common with \nsummertime convection, a few storms could become strong to even \nsevere each afternoon with gusty winds, frequent lightning, and \nlocally heavy rainfall.",
